WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.015 --> 00:00:17.384
We're going to pick up

00:00:17.384 --> 00:00:20.387
where we left off on Monday.

00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:23.289
And where we left off was
we were dissolving stuff.

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:26.459
We talked about how
things dissolve.

00:00:26.459 --> 00:00:28.361
Why does something
dissolve maybe

00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:29.996
in this solvent versus that?

00:00:29.996 --> 00:00:31.131
And how does it dissolve?

00:00:31.131 --> 00:00:32.131
And what makes them--

00:00:32.131 --> 00:00:34.300
and then we say, well,
what if we had something

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.336
and we wanted it to dissolve
all the way until it

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:37.470
couldn't dissolve anymore?

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:41.641
Well, that's called
saturation, right?

00:00:41.641 --> 00:00:42.776
And we talked about that.

00:00:42.776 --> 00:00:46.312
We talked about how if you
had a general reaction, maybe

00:00:46.312 --> 00:00:52.619
we had something
like this, aA plus bB

00:00:52.619 --> 00:00:56.099
goes to and comes
back from

00:00:56.099 --> 00:01:01.161
cC plus dD,
then this reaction

00:01:01.161 --> 00:01:03.063
can happen in both ways, right?

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:07.934
And so the general concept that
we called a reaction quotient,

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:13.039
reaction Q, is equal to--

00:01:13.039 --> 00:01:16.443
remember, it's equal
to the concentrations

00:01:16.443 --> 00:01:20.646
raised to those
stoichiometric coefficients.

00:01:20.646 --> 00:01:25.318
And it's the products
over the reactants.

00:01:25.318 --> 00:01:26.719
OK, so that's what we--

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:33.226
now then, we say,
well OK, that's

00:01:33.226 --> 00:01:36.963
a thing that has
to do with maybe--

00:01:36.963 --> 00:01:38.598
where's that reaction going?

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:40.633
What's dominating it?

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:43.970
And remember, these are the
stoichiometric coefficients,

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:47.240
and they're related to
literally the probability

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.576
that that reaction can happen.

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:52.979
OK, and so if you
want an intuitive

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:56.015
reason for the exponents, you
can think about this as, well,

00:01:56.015 --> 00:01:59.252
if these all have to get
close enough in a given volume

00:01:59.252 --> 00:02:04.357
to react, then that's where
those exponents come from.

00:02:04.357 --> 00:02:06.192
But unlike the rate law--

00:02:06.192 --> 00:02:08.495
and I said this Monday, and
I'm saying it again today--

00:02:08.495 --> 00:02:10.896
they are not things that
are experimentally measured.

00:02:10.896 --> 00:02:15.268
Instead, they just come from
the reaction itself, OK?

00:02:15.268 --> 00:02:18.771
So we're just getting back
into the right mood here.

00:02:18.771 --> 00:02:20.173
But then we said,
well, but look.

00:02:20.173 --> 00:02:21.508
OK, this thing is reacting.

00:02:21.508 --> 00:02:23.009
It's going back and
forth, whatever.

00:02:23.009 --> 00:02:26.880
But what about once it
reaches equilibrium,

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.047
all right, equilibrium?

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:31.284
And when it reaches equilibrium,
it's the same thing,

00:02:31.284 --> 00:02:38.291
but we call it Keq,
in equilibrium,

00:02:38.291 --> 00:02:41.761
eq'm, equilibrium, right?

00:02:41.761 --> 00:02:46.031
So that reaction quotient
has a very specific value,

00:02:46.031 --> 00:02:52.338
a constant, a constant,
once this way is happening

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:55.241
in the same amount as that way.

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:58.711
Right, that's the
saturation point.

00:02:58.711 --> 00:03:00.480
Well, if you're
dissolving something,

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:03.550
that's the saturation point.

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:06.252
OK, so it's where the
precipitation-- remember,

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:09.289
we wrote this as dissolution
and precipitation

00:03:09.289 --> 00:03:11.024
when we were putting
stuff in solution.

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:12.959
And that's where we got to.

00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:15.562
And we went a
little bit further,

00:03:15.562 --> 00:03:17.263
and we had a specific example.

00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:18.898
And that's where I
want to start today,

00:03:18.898 --> 00:03:21.234
and that's silver chloride.

00:03:21.234 --> 00:03:22.468
OK, so here we go.

00:03:22.468 --> 00:03:25.171
So we have silver chloride.

00:03:25.171 --> 00:03:29.909
And if I write this all out for
silver chloride, I've got AgCl.

00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:32.745
And I've got a
little pinch of it,

00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:34.480
like a little speck
of silver chloride.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.382
And I'm putting
it into a beaker.

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:41.321
So it starts out as a solid,
and I add some water to it.

00:03:41.321 --> 00:03:44.457
And the water is l,
because it's everywhere.

00:03:44.457 --> 00:03:45.124
It's the liquid.

00:03:45.124 --> 00:03:46.459
It's the solvent.

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:49.529
OK, it's the liquid that
I'm dissolving it in.

00:03:49.529 --> 00:03:52.131
And we say, well OK, that's
going to go like this.

00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:56.936
So that reaction, it's going
to give us silver ions--

00:03:56.936 --> 00:04:00.273
remember salts, we did salts--

00:04:00.273 --> 00:04:08.014
and chlorine ions, and
back to some more water.

00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:09.782
OK, that's H2O.

00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:13.386
Now, the aq means aqueous.

00:04:13.386 --> 00:04:16.289
Because it's a
salt, right, these

00:04:16.289 --> 00:04:19.358
are now dissolved ions,
because it's a salt.

00:04:19.358 --> 00:04:20.893
So we write them like that.

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:23.997
And we know we got
water everywhere.

00:04:23.997 --> 00:04:28.568
So oftentimes, we
leave the water out.

00:04:28.568 --> 00:04:30.403
Oftentimes we don't
really write the water,

00:04:30.403 --> 00:04:31.971
because it's on both sides.

00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:36.909
And anyway, the water is
just surrounding stuff.

00:04:36.909 --> 00:04:39.712
It's not necessarily
being consumed.

00:04:39.712 --> 00:04:42.982
The concentration of the
water isn't changing.

00:04:42.982 --> 00:04:45.852
The water is the water
in the liquid phase.

00:04:45.852 --> 00:04:48.888
Well, OK, then we went
even further and said,

00:04:48.888 --> 00:04:53.860
well, if you write
a Keq for this, then

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:57.430
that would look something like
the concentration of silver

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:03.236
plus in solution times the
concentration of chlorine

00:05:03.236 --> 00:05:05.738
minus in solution.

00:05:05.738 --> 00:05:09.008
And that would be divided
by the concentration

00:05:09.008 --> 00:05:12.679
of silver chloride as a solid.

00:05:12.679 --> 00:05:14.947
And then we said--

00:05:14.947 --> 00:05:17.082
but hold on.

00:05:17.082 --> 00:05:19.118
But hold on.

00:05:19.118 --> 00:05:22.188
In the solid phase,
the concentration

00:05:22.188 --> 00:05:23.523
isn't changing, right?

00:05:23.523 --> 00:05:27.193
As a solid, the concentration
of silver chloride

00:05:27.193 --> 00:05:28.061
is silver chloride.

00:05:28.061 --> 00:05:29.796
It's a constant.

00:05:29.796 --> 00:05:34.067
And so then we said, well
OK, if this is a constant,

00:05:34.067 --> 00:05:35.835
this is a constant.

00:05:38.938 --> 00:05:45.244
Then we can also absorb it
into the equilibrium constant.

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:47.013
And I somewhat
confusingly wrote it

00:05:47.013 --> 00:05:51.851
as Ksp in both this
form and the form

00:05:51.851 --> 00:05:53.853
that we know and
love for Ksp, which

00:05:53.853 --> 00:05:57.890
is simply literally the
solubilities of these times

00:05:57.890 --> 00:05:59.192
each other.

00:05:59.192 --> 00:06:01.260
Right, and that is Ksp.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.096
So I wanted just
to clarify this,

00:06:03.096 --> 00:06:06.833
because a student asked a
very good question, which is,

00:06:06.833 --> 00:06:08.468
which is it?

00:06:08.468 --> 00:06:11.170
Is this in Ksp or
is this in Keq?

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:17.009
Well, the big full picture for
Keq, you'd write it all in.

00:06:17.009 --> 00:06:25.184
But the solubility product is
focusing in on the dissolution

00:06:25.184 --> 00:06:27.120
of these ions.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:30.022
And so you wrap this into
the equilibrium constant,

00:06:30.022 --> 00:06:31.491
because it's another constant.

00:06:31.491 --> 00:06:33.126
So that's the
solubility product.

00:06:33.126 --> 00:06:35.161
And that's where we
got to on Monday.

00:06:35.161 --> 00:06:39.665
The solubility product is a
special equilibrium constant,

00:06:39.665 --> 00:06:44.837
where what we're
talking about is--

00:06:44.837 --> 00:06:47.306
what are the
concentrations when you've

00:06:47.306 --> 00:06:52.044
reached saturation of this
dissolution precipitation

00:06:52.044 --> 00:06:53.846
reaction, OK?

00:06:53.846 --> 00:06:55.615
That's the solubility product.

00:06:55.615 --> 00:06:57.283
That's Ksp.

00:06:57.283 --> 00:06:59.852
So you wrap this
in, and you get Ksp.

00:07:02.555 --> 00:07:04.123
You can think about
it as, well, maybe

00:07:04.123 --> 00:07:08.728
you could have called this
some Ksp in a pre-Ksp.

00:07:08.728 --> 00:07:10.396
And then you wrap
this in, and then it's

00:07:10.396 --> 00:07:12.965
still just [INAUDIBLE],, because
it's still just a constant

00:07:12.965 --> 00:07:14.367
representing this reaction.

00:07:14.367 --> 00:07:16.302
OK, I just wanted to
make this super clear,

00:07:16.302 --> 00:07:19.672
because this part leads
to confusion often,

00:07:19.672 --> 00:07:22.241
that this gets left out,
because it's constant.

00:07:22.241 --> 00:07:23.543
It doesn't change.

00:07:23.543 --> 00:07:25.244
OK, good.

00:07:25.244 --> 00:07:29.582
Now we get to that,
because this is a constant.

00:07:29.582 --> 00:07:31.784
OK, so what does that mean?

00:07:31.784 --> 00:07:35.621
Well, it means that if I
dissolve this thing in water,

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:42.028
then the equilibrium, the
equilibrium is a fixed number.

00:07:42.028 --> 00:07:44.063
This equilibrium
product is fixed.

00:07:44.063 --> 00:07:46.199
And that has a very
important meaning.

00:07:46.199 --> 00:07:47.567
It's the green curve.

00:07:47.567 --> 00:07:50.069
You see, it says equilibrium.

00:07:50.069 --> 00:07:54.340
And so what that means is that
if I'm at point B, well, there

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:55.041
I go.

00:07:55.041 --> 00:07:58.144
I've got my-- we said Ksp.

00:07:58.144 --> 00:08:08.588
Ksp for this one equals,
what was it, 1.7 times 10

00:08:08.588 --> 00:08:11.057
to the minus 10th.

00:08:11.057 --> 00:08:13.693
Units are coming.

00:08:13.693 --> 00:08:24.737
And so that's the Ksp for
silver chloride and H20.

00:08:24.737 --> 00:08:30.877
Oh, and you have to say
at some temperature.

00:08:30.877 --> 00:08:33.246
It will depend on temperature.

00:08:33.246 --> 00:08:37.415
You often will just be at room
temperature, 25C, for example.

00:08:37.415 --> 00:08:40.019
So often these get quoted
just at room temperature.

00:08:42.889 --> 00:08:46.292
OK, so once I know that, then
I know that that's equilibrium

00:08:46.292 --> 00:08:47.660
no matter what, right?

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:48.895
And so that means that if--

00:08:48.895 --> 00:08:50.029
and then we solve this.

00:08:50.029 --> 00:08:54.901
You said, well OK, if I
dissolve silver chloride,

00:08:54.901 --> 00:08:59.672
and I had nothing
else, then the Ksp

00:08:59.672 --> 00:09:03.910
would equal the
concentration of silver

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:08.614
times the concentration
of chlorine ions.

00:09:08.614 --> 00:09:09.782
But those are equal.

00:09:09.782 --> 00:09:11.284
And so we let each
one of them be x,

00:09:11.284 --> 00:09:13.119
then it's equal to x squared.

00:09:13.119 --> 00:09:18.524
And so we know that the
concentration of each of them

00:09:18.524 --> 00:09:22.528
is 10 to the minus--

00:09:22.528 --> 00:09:23.930
let me write it exactly--

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:26.599
1.3 times 10 to the minus fifth.

00:09:26.599 --> 00:09:28.467
Now, it's a
concentration, so you

00:09:28.467 --> 00:09:32.805
know that it's got to be
capital M, moles per liter.

00:09:32.805 --> 00:09:35.241
And since that's the
case, since that's

00:09:35.241 --> 00:09:38.210
where you know what it is,
then now you can back up.

00:09:38.210 --> 00:09:41.013
Because this is the
concentration of Ag ions

00:09:41.013 --> 00:09:43.549
and it's also the
concentration of chlorine ions,

00:09:43.549 --> 00:09:46.652
then you know that
the units of Ksp

00:09:46.652 --> 00:09:49.088
must be M squared, all right?

00:09:52.091 --> 00:10:01.100
Units in this case,
moles per liter squared.

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:04.303
Must be, because otherwise
the concentration isn't right,

00:10:04.303 --> 00:10:06.772
the units of concentration.

00:10:06.772 --> 00:10:08.641
OK, now we're going
to play with this.

00:10:08.641 --> 00:10:12.311
OK, so I'm at B. I'm at
point B. You see that?

00:10:12.311 --> 00:10:13.379
There I am.

00:10:13.379 --> 00:10:15.581
And I've got my
concentration of chlorine,

00:10:15.581 --> 00:10:16.882
concentration of silver.

00:10:16.882 --> 00:10:18.517
And I'm here.

00:10:18.517 --> 00:10:21.654
And so that's 1.3 times
10 to the minus fifth.

00:10:21.654 --> 00:10:25.191
OK, now if I didn't
have as much,

00:10:25.191 --> 00:10:29.862
if I weren't at the saturation
point, then I could add more.

00:10:29.862 --> 00:10:31.364
I'd be here.

00:10:31.364 --> 00:10:33.933
And I could add more
solid silver chloride.

00:10:33.933 --> 00:10:39.438
And eventually, I'd
reach this equilibrium.

00:10:39.438 --> 00:10:42.308
But if I added more
than that, so now I'm

00:10:42.308 --> 00:10:46.979
adding way more than I can, then
it's just going to be a solid.

00:10:46.979 --> 00:10:48.848
And that's kind of boring.

00:10:48.848 --> 00:10:50.750
So now I'm up here.

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:52.785
But maybe I add ions in.

00:10:52.785 --> 00:10:53.719
So these are the ions.

00:10:53.719 --> 00:10:55.287
So maybe I add a
whole bunch of ions.

00:10:55.287 --> 00:10:56.922
Well, they're just
going to precipitate.

00:10:56.922 --> 00:10:58.924
I can't be here and
be in equilibrium.

00:10:58.924 --> 00:11:00.693
That's the point
of the green curve.

00:11:00.693 --> 00:11:03.029
This K doesn't change.

00:11:03.029 --> 00:11:08.200
This K is this K. That's
what the green curve is.

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:11.270
It is that being constant.

00:11:11.270 --> 00:11:12.171
That's very important.

00:11:12.171 --> 00:11:13.205
That is equilibrium.

00:11:13.205 --> 00:11:19.578
Now, there is a way though
to move around on it,

00:11:19.578 --> 00:11:21.914
because if I did, imagine now--

00:11:21.914 --> 00:11:24.950
look at point C.
Imagine now that I've

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:28.988
found some source
of chlorine ions

00:11:28.988 --> 00:11:33.659
and I add a whole bunch of them,
right, a whole bunch of them

00:11:33.659 --> 00:11:35.094
into the solution.

00:11:35.094 --> 00:11:37.129
I don't want to add silver
and chlorine together.

00:11:37.129 --> 00:11:39.065
I just add chlorine.

00:11:39.065 --> 00:11:40.766
Well, you can see
what's going to happen.

00:11:40.766 --> 00:11:42.802
I have to get to equilibrium.

00:11:42.802 --> 00:11:46.639
And so in order to stay in
equilibrium for silver chloride

00:11:46.639 --> 00:11:50.643
in solution, then I must lower
my concentration of silver,

00:11:50.643 --> 00:11:53.813
because I added
all this chlorine.

00:11:53.813 --> 00:11:55.915
The only way to do
that is to eat up

00:11:55.915 --> 00:11:58.250
some silver and precipitate.

00:11:58.250 --> 00:11:59.685
That's the only way to do that.

00:11:59.685 --> 00:12:01.387
And that has a name.

00:12:01.387 --> 00:12:03.289
And that's what I want
to talk about next.

00:12:03.289 --> 00:12:05.725
That is called the
common ion effect.

00:12:09.695 --> 00:12:12.665
Now, this is an example.

00:12:12.665 --> 00:12:16.902
This is an example of
a broader principle

00:12:16.902 --> 00:12:22.041
which I mentioned Monday, which
is Le Chatelier's principle.

00:12:22.041 --> 00:12:22.541
OK.

00:12:27.646 --> 00:12:39.191
And that is that position
of equilibrium will move.

00:12:39.191 --> 00:12:43.629
It's still equilibrium, but
it's moving on that green curve

00:12:43.629 --> 00:12:45.064
to counteract change.

00:12:50.069 --> 00:12:54.974
This is a very
general principle.

00:12:54.974 --> 00:12:58.978
It applies to many, many
things, not just concentrations.

00:12:58.978 --> 00:13:01.814
It applies to changes in
pressure and other changes

00:13:01.814 --> 00:13:02.915
that you make in a system.

00:13:02.915 --> 00:13:04.750
Here we care about
concentration.

00:13:04.750 --> 00:13:07.186
And in this
particular case, we're

00:13:07.186 --> 00:13:08.721
going to see it for
what happens when

00:13:08.721 --> 00:13:14.426
you add ions of one particular
type and not the other.

00:13:14.426 --> 00:13:18.130
That's the common ion effect,
but it's a very general effect,

00:13:18.130 --> 00:13:20.232
that it resists the change.

00:13:20.232 --> 00:13:23.435
Now, let's see how that works.

00:13:26.772 --> 00:13:29.141
If I want a source
of ions, let's say

00:13:29.141 --> 00:13:31.544
I want a source of
chlorine, one way

00:13:31.544 --> 00:13:34.847
would be to add a whole
bunch of sodium chloride.

00:13:34.847 --> 00:13:38.784
So let's suppose that I have--

00:13:38.784 --> 00:13:46.859
OK, so as an example, I'm going
to add to this nice equilibrium

00:13:46.859 --> 00:13:49.695
that I reached here--
so I'm at point B, OK?

00:13:49.695 --> 00:14:00.506
And I'm going to add 0.1 moles
per liter of sodium chloride.

00:14:00.506 --> 00:14:06.045
And this nearly-- and so
we're going to say it does--

00:14:06.045 --> 00:14:10.649
it nearly fully disassociates.

00:14:10.649 --> 00:14:13.185
And we'll be talking
about that later today.

00:14:13.185 --> 00:14:22.661
And so that means that NaCl
basically goes to Na+ plus Cl-,

00:14:22.661 --> 00:14:23.829
both in solution.

00:14:23.829 --> 00:14:32.071
So if I put 0.1 moles per
liter of sodium chloride

00:14:32.071 --> 00:14:35.674
into this container of
the silver chloride,

00:14:35.674 --> 00:14:37.476
I'm basically going
to get 0.1, because

00:14:37.476 --> 00:14:38.844
of the stoichiometric
coefficient

00:14:38.844 --> 00:14:41.847
here, 1, 1, 1, right?

00:14:41.847 --> 00:14:43.649
So if I put 0.1 moles
per liter of this,

00:14:43.649 --> 00:14:46.852
I'm going to have 0.1 moles
per liter of chlorine ions

00:14:46.852 --> 00:14:49.822
that I just dumped
into the container.

00:14:49.822 --> 00:14:53.859
But the sodium is not
going to do anything.

00:14:53.859 --> 00:14:55.294
But look at the chlorine.

00:14:55.294 --> 00:14:59.398
That's going to mess
with my equilibrium.

00:14:59.398 --> 00:15:01.267
But K is a constant.

00:15:01.267 --> 00:15:03.102
So let's see what happens.

00:15:03.102 --> 00:15:05.170
We can do it visually.

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:08.340
But we can also do it back when
we use the ICE table, right?

00:15:08.340 --> 00:15:12.745
So now we're going to
go ICE again, AgCl.

00:15:12.745 --> 00:15:15.648
And here's Ag+.

00:15:18.183 --> 00:15:20.219
And here's Cl-.

00:15:20.219 --> 00:15:21.887
So now I've added the--

00:15:21.887 --> 00:15:25.057
OK, so my initial is
that this is solid.

00:15:25.057 --> 00:15:29.361
And remember, this is the solid
that I'm putting in there.

00:15:29.361 --> 00:15:33.632
And then my initial
is that I started

00:15:33.632 --> 00:15:35.434
in that nice
equilibrium at point B.

00:15:35.434 --> 00:15:39.204
So I've got 1.3 times
10 to the minus fifth.

00:15:39.204 --> 00:15:40.873
And over here,
I've got the same.

00:15:44.343 --> 00:15:47.146
But now the change,
now the change.

00:15:49.648 --> 00:15:55.220
OK, so we're going to add
some chlorine ions, 0.1.

00:15:55.220 --> 00:15:57.723
So I've added 0.1 moles
per liter of chlorine ions

00:15:57.723 --> 00:16:00.726
from the salt, from
sodium chloride.

00:16:00.726 --> 00:16:05.364
Yeah, but now some of
that is going to react,

00:16:05.364 --> 00:16:08.167
because I just said, Le
Chatelier's principle

00:16:08.167 --> 00:16:11.637
tells me that now it's
got to counter that.

00:16:11.637 --> 00:16:13.572
And you can see
it right here too.

00:16:13.572 --> 00:16:15.774
I'm adding a whole
bunch of chlorine.

00:16:15.774 --> 00:16:17.042
Look at how far I'm going out.

00:16:17.042 --> 00:16:22.781
0.1, that's literally
all the way out to here.

00:16:22.781 --> 00:16:25.951
And so the amount of
silver has got to go down.

00:16:25.951 --> 00:16:28.220
And what's going
to happen is it's

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:31.590
going to consume some of the
silver and form precipitate

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:34.026
to counter all this addition.

00:16:34.026 --> 00:16:37.496
It's going to run the
reaction the other way.

00:16:37.496 --> 00:16:42.067
So it's going to lose a
little bit there from the 0.1.

00:16:42.067 --> 00:16:44.803
That's going to react
with the silver ions.

00:16:44.803 --> 00:16:49.241
So that's going to also lose,
and this is going to gain.

00:16:49.241 --> 00:16:50.809
That's how much
that's going to gain.

00:16:50.809 --> 00:16:52.211
So those are the
variables, right?

00:16:52.211 --> 00:16:54.013
This is the ICE table.

00:16:54.013 --> 00:16:55.914
That's how much
it's going to lo--

00:16:55.914 --> 00:16:58.951
these are going to react to
give me some precipitate.

00:16:58.951 --> 00:17:06.425
And so the equilibrium
is more solid forms.

00:17:06.425 --> 00:17:10.061
That's not a good
E. More solid forms.

00:17:10.061 --> 00:17:16.734
And over here, it's 1.3 times
10 to the minus fifth minus x.

00:17:16.734 --> 00:17:21.306
And over here, it's 0.1 minus x.

00:17:21.306 --> 00:17:23.976
That's the
equilibrium condition.

00:17:23.976 --> 00:17:25.644
I've added in a common ion.

00:17:25.644 --> 00:17:28.380
This is the common ion effect.

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:35.054
Yeah, so now if you do the
math, then what happens?

00:17:35.054 --> 00:17:39.625
Well again, Ksp is the same.

00:17:39.625 --> 00:17:40.993
It's the equilibrium constant.

00:17:40.993 --> 00:17:41.994
So it's the constant.

00:17:41.994 --> 00:17:44.997
So that is going to
be the same value.

00:17:44.997 --> 00:17:50.669
It's going to be 1.7 times
10 to the minus 10th.

00:17:50.669 --> 00:17:54.306
But you see, Ksp is also
equal to the concentration

00:17:54.306 --> 00:17:57.776
in equilibrium of the silver
ions times the concentration

00:17:57.776 --> 00:17:58.911
of the chlorine ions.

00:17:58.911 --> 00:18:05.050
So it's going to be 0.1
minus x times 1.3 times 10

00:18:05.050 --> 00:18:07.453
to the fifth minus x.

00:18:10.022 --> 00:18:14.026
And then we don't like doing all
this math, and so we simplify.

00:18:14.026 --> 00:18:17.229
Then we can simplify,
because look,

00:18:17.229 --> 00:18:21.600
you started with silver ions
in a very small concentration.

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:25.037
You can't take away
something you don't have.

00:18:25.037 --> 00:18:30.209
And so x has got to be
somehow this or less, right?

00:18:30.209 --> 00:18:32.144
And so x is a small number.

00:18:32.144 --> 00:18:35.314
And this is a really big
number in comparison.

00:18:35.314 --> 00:18:39.151
And so we like simplifying
our lives, right?

00:18:39.151 --> 00:18:42.488
And so we like saying
that that's like 0.1.

00:18:42.488 --> 00:18:43.455
We know that.

00:18:43.455 --> 00:18:46.024
That'll make the
math a lot easier.

00:18:46.024 --> 00:18:48.026
It'll make the math easier.

00:18:48.026 --> 00:18:58.770
And so now we can say that this
is equal to 0.1 times 1.3 times

00:18:58.770 --> 00:19:01.940
10 to the minus fifth minus x.

00:19:01.940 --> 00:19:06.712
And then we get that
x equals 1.7 times 10

00:19:06.712 --> 00:19:08.747
to the minus ninth.

00:19:08.747 --> 00:19:10.649
That is the common ion effect.

00:19:10.649 --> 00:19:12.985
What I've done, I've taken
this thing in equilibrium,

00:19:12.985 --> 00:19:14.286
and I've added one of the ions.

00:19:18.123 --> 00:19:20.492
And it changes the solubility.

00:19:20.492 --> 00:19:23.195
Remember, the solubility
is what we care about.

00:19:23.195 --> 00:19:27.766
That's what the solubility
constant helps us determine.

00:19:27.766 --> 00:19:32.938
And I've literally just changed
it by orders of magnitude,

00:19:32.938 --> 00:19:36.041
because now the
amount that this--

00:19:36.041 --> 00:19:38.443
so this thing
precipitates like crazy.

00:19:38.443 --> 00:19:41.914
I added in a little bit
of some other salt that

00:19:41.914 --> 00:19:42.748
had chlorine ions.

00:19:42.748 --> 00:19:46.618
And all of a sudden, silver
chloride precipitates out.

00:19:46.618 --> 00:19:47.719
So here's a video of this.

00:19:47.719 --> 00:19:48.453
This is kind of cool.

00:19:48.453 --> 00:19:49.054
So watch.

00:19:49.054 --> 00:19:51.690
This is an
equilibrium container.

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:54.893
And what I'm adding is
salt. And look at that.

00:19:54.893 --> 00:19:55.460
What is that?

00:19:55.460 --> 00:19:58.730
That's silver chloride
precipitate, right,

00:19:58.730 --> 00:20:01.300
because the thing has
to reach equilibrium.

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:03.969
And so in order to
do so, what you do

00:20:03.969 --> 00:20:06.471
is you suppress the solubility.

00:20:06.471 --> 00:20:10.175
Literally, by adding chlorine,
the common ion effect,

00:20:10.175 --> 00:20:13.745
you suppress the solubility
of the silver chloride.

00:20:13.745 --> 00:20:16.148
It's pretty cool stuff.

00:20:16.148 --> 00:20:17.382
That is the common ion effect.

00:20:17.382 --> 00:20:19.718
Now, why does this matter?

00:20:19.718 --> 00:20:21.420
Let me give you one--
no, I'll get to why

00:20:21.420 --> 00:20:22.554
this matters in a second.

00:20:22.554 --> 00:20:24.122
One more question.

00:20:24.122 --> 00:20:27.292
Now without even doing the
math, check this one out.

00:20:27.292 --> 00:20:29.695
This is cool.

00:20:29.695 --> 00:20:34.266
Without even doing the math,
I can take barium sulfate,

00:20:34.266 --> 00:20:39.137
and from the common ion
effect-- so barium sulfate is

00:20:39.137 --> 00:20:39.771
going to go.

00:20:39.771 --> 00:20:41.106
So what does that one look like?

00:20:41.106 --> 00:20:49.248
Well, it's going to go to Ba 2+
plus SO4 2-, both in solution,

00:20:49.248 --> 00:20:50.349
in solution.

00:20:50.349 --> 00:20:53.385
Dissolved ions, this is a solid.

00:20:53.385 --> 00:20:57.189
Now, here's the question.

00:20:57.189 --> 00:21:00.325
Which of the following will be
required in the least amount

00:21:00.325 --> 00:21:05.130
to dissolve the same
amount of BaSO4?

00:21:05.130 --> 00:21:07.266
I don't need any
constants or math.

00:21:07.266 --> 00:21:11.136
I can use this same principle,
because if I had some 0.1

00:21:11.136 --> 00:21:15.040
molar or 0.01 moles per
liter of either of these,

00:21:15.040 --> 00:21:16.575
what are they doing?

00:21:16.575 --> 00:21:19.611
They're serving up ions, right?

00:21:22.281 --> 00:21:24.149
In the one case,
you're serving up--

00:21:24.149 --> 00:21:28.320
so in case A, you're
going to give me--

00:21:28.320 --> 00:21:29.421
what do I have there?

00:21:29.421 --> 00:21:33.358
H2SO4, so let's do A first.

00:21:33.358 --> 00:21:41.400
H2SO4 is going to give me a
whole bunch of H+ but also SO4

00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:42.567
2-.

00:21:42.567 --> 00:21:48.206
And in case B, you have BaCl--

00:21:48.206 --> 00:21:51.910
OK, so you have BaCl2.

00:21:51.910 --> 00:21:55.447
And if I put that in, I've
got this in water, right?

00:21:55.447 --> 00:21:57.482
But if this dissolves
in water a little bit,

00:21:57.482 --> 00:22:02.654
then it's going to serve
up some Ba 2+ plus 2Cl-.

00:22:05.791 --> 00:22:09.494
Either way, now you know
what's going to happen,

00:22:09.494 --> 00:22:12.964
because if I'm trying
to dissolve something

00:22:12.964 --> 00:22:17.102
and I add any of the
ions that it's dissolving

00:22:17.102 --> 00:22:19.638
into from some
external source, it's

00:22:19.638 --> 00:22:21.973
going to drive it this way.

00:22:21.973 --> 00:22:25.310
It's going to precipitate.

00:22:25.310 --> 00:22:27.579
So the answer has
to be just water.

00:22:27.579 --> 00:22:31.049
Stick with pure
water in this case.

00:22:31.049 --> 00:22:33.318
Otherwise you're going to
have more trouble dissolving,

00:22:33.318 --> 00:22:34.686
just like we just showed.

00:22:34.686 --> 00:22:36.988
You're going to have more
trouble dissolving, not less.

00:22:36.988 --> 00:22:38.190
That's the common ion effect.

00:22:40.992 --> 00:22:44.062
OK, so now why does this matter?

00:22:44.062 --> 00:22:46.365
Why does this matter?

00:22:46.365 --> 00:22:48.433
We go back to the pteropod.

00:22:48.433 --> 00:22:50.102
And by the way, I
didn't have this link,

00:22:50.102 --> 00:22:52.804
and I should have when
I showed you the--

00:22:52.804 --> 00:22:54.706
and this is your
goody bag, et cetera.

00:22:54.706 --> 00:22:56.308
There's some really
nice articles

00:22:56.308 --> 00:22:58.643
here that you can find
related to these experiments

00:22:58.643 --> 00:23:01.513
and other things about
ocean acidity in case

00:23:01.513 --> 00:23:03.348
you're interested.

00:23:03.348 --> 00:23:06.785
But see, what I did
was that this was my

00:23:06.785 --> 00:23:08.320
why this matters on Monday.

00:23:08.320 --> 00:23:11.690
And I wanted to tell you
about the goody bag and about

00:23:11.690 --> 00:23:16.595
how things dissolve, because
Monday was about dissolving

00:23:16.595 --> 00:23:19.030
and finding a saturation point.

00:23:19.030 --> 00:23:22.167
Now we can get to the
next place, which is,

00:23:22.167 --> 00:23:25.971
why does that matter for
the pteropod's shell?

00:23:25.971 --> 00:23:29.307
What is the chemistry
that matters there?

00:23:29.307 --> 00:23:32.778
OK, so I made the ocean
a little more acidic.

00:23:32.778 --> 00:23:34.012
Why does that matter?

00:23:34.012 --> 00:23:36.415
But you see, now we're
armed with the knowledge we

00:23:36.415 --> 00:23:37.849
need to answer that question.

00:23:37.849 --> 00:23:38.850
Now we're armed with it.

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:42.120
All we need to do, as always
with everything in life,

00:23:42.120 --> 00:23:43.822
is look at the chemistry.

00:23:43.822 --> 00:23:45.490
That's it.

00:23:45.490 --> 00:23:48.226
Say that at the
Thanksgiving table.

00:23:48.226 --> 00:23:50.095
You'll be very popular.

00:23:50.095 --> 00:23:53.565
We said CO2 plus H2O.

00:23:53.565 --> 00:24:01.606
This goes-- oh, find some
equilibrium to H2CO3.

00:24:01.606 --> 00:24:03.308
Now, this is called
carbonic acid.

00:24:09.047 --> 00:24:10.582
This is called carbonic acid.

00:24:10.582 --> 00:24:12.317
There's the pteropod up there.

00:24:12.317 --> 00:24:14.453
And there's the reaction
that's really relevant

00:24:14.453 --> 00:24:16.788
that you'll see from the ones
we're about to write down.

00:24:16.788 --> 00:24:18.056
Why?

00:24:18.056 --> 00:24:22.260
Because the thing is that,
what happens to carbonic acid?

00:24:22.260 --> 00:24:28.400
Well, carbonic acid also goes
through a dissolution reaction.

00:24:28.400 --> 00:24:32.204
So this is CO2
dissolving in water.

00:24:32.204 --> 00:24:36.741
So carbonic acid goes like this.

00:24:36.741 --> 00:24:42.914
OK, it goes into HCO3- plus H+.

00:24:46.685 --> 00:24:48.286
Now, here's the thing.

00:24:48.286 --> 00:24:50.388
OK, what is the shell made of?

00:24:50.388 --> 00:24:57.395
Well, the shell that's
dissolving, the core material

00:24:57.395 --> 00:24:59.064
is calcium carbonate.

00:24:59.064 --> 00:25:02.167
So that's CaCO3.

00:25:02.167 --> 00:25:03.301
That's the shell.

00:25:03.301 --> 00:25:07.606
And the shell also has
an equilibrium reaction

00:25:07.606 --> 00:25:08.974
that happens.

00:25:08.974 --> 00:25:13.945
The shell of a sea creature
is in dynamic equilibrium

00:25:13.945 --> 00:25:15.514
with the ocean.

00:25:15.514 --> 00:25:17.082
And so it's going like this.

00:25:17.082 --> 00:25:27.359
It's going to, well,
OK, Ca2+ and CO3 2-.

00:25:27.359 --> 00:25:30.328
OK, but the thing is, it
has an equilibrium constant.

00:25:30.328 --> 00:25:33.598
All these have
equilibrium constants.

00:25:33.598 --> 00:25:37.502
So for example, for
this one, the Ksp,

00:25:37.502 --> 00:25:39.237
the solubility
product constant--

00:25:39.237 --> 00:25:40.438
because this is a solid.

00:25:40.438 --> 00:25:43.508
This is the solid
shell, solid, going

00:25:43.508 --> 00:25:45.076
to ions in aqueous solution.

00:25:47.812 --> 00:25:52.183
So the Ksp for that is
somewhere around 5 times 10

00:25:52.183 --> 00:25:55.987
to the minus ninth, 5 times
10 to the minus ninth.

00:25:55.987 --> 00:25:57.122
Yeah, but here's the thing.

00:25:57.122 --> 00:25:59.391
We just went through this.

00:25:59.391 --> 00:26:03.061
If I consume one of these or
change the concentration of one

00:26:03.061 --> 00:26:06.731
of these and not the other--

00:26:06.731 --> 00:26:07.566
we just did this.

00:26:07.566 --> 00:26:09.367
If I could change
the concentration,

00:26:09.367 --> 00:26:14.005
if I consume any of these, then
I might drive the reaction--

00:26:14.005 --> 00:26:15.740
consume or produce.

00:26:15.740 --> 00:26:18.543
If I change any of
these independently,

00:26:18.543 --> 00:26:21.813
I'm going to drive the
reaction, because that's

00:26:21.813 --> 00:26:25.817
how we keep to our K.
That's what Le Chatelier's

00:26:25.817 --> 00:26:27.352
principle tells us.

00:26:27.352 --> 00:26:36.661
And so what ends up happening
is you've got the extra H+ ions.

00:26:36.661 --> 00:26:38.663
So these are ions in solution.

00:26:38.663 --> 00:26:39.698
Where did they come from?

00:26:39.698 --> 00:26:43.168
They came from the CO2
giving us carbonic acid,

00:26:43.168 --> 00:26:44.936
which then gave us H+.

00:26:44.936 --> 00:26:46.871
Those are what
I'm talking about.

00:26:46.871 --> 00:26:54.012
Well, they react
with the CO3 2-.

00:26:57.616 --> 00:27:03.288
This lowers the CO3 2-
concentration near the shell--

00:27:03.288 --> 00:27:03.989
[STUDENT SNEEZES]

00:27:03.989 --> 00:27:04.689
--right?

00:27:04.689 --> 00:27:05.290
Gesundheit.

00:27:05.290 --> 00:27:07.926
And if I lower this, because
I've taken some of this now

00:27:07.926 --> 00:27:11.262
and I've reacted it, so
now I've got less of it.

00:27:11.262 --> 00:27:13.765
And because of what we just
saw, if I've got less of this,

00:27:13.765 --> 00:27:17.135
you're going to drive
this way, which is going

00:27:17.135 --> 00:27:20.839
to dissolve more of the shell.

00:27:20.839 --> 00:27:22.374
That's why this works.

00:27:22.374 --> 00:27:25.777
Well, that's why this happens.

00:27:25.777 --> 00:27:29.581
Works sounds like
a positive thing.

00:27:29.581 --> 00:27:37.489
So this lowers the
concentration of CO3 2-,

00:27:37.489 --> 00:27:44.796
and that drives
more dissolution.

00:27:44.796 --> 00:27:46.231
That is what's happening.

00:27:46.231 --> 00:27:48.967
And we now can understand
it in terms of the concepts

00:27:48.967 --> 00:27:51.936
that we've just learned.

00:27:51.936 --> 00:27:53.872
Historically, I mentioned
50 million years.

00:27:53.872 --> 00:27:56.341
Actually, by some
accounts, it's 300 million.

00:27:56.341 --> 00:28:00.178
It depends on which
studies you read.

00:28:00.178 --> 00:28:03.615
But for at least 50 and maybe
as much as 300 million years,

00:28:03.615 --> 00:28:08.620
the ocean has had a pH of 8.18.

00:28:08.620 --> 00:28:12.157
Now, where's the-- oh.

00:28:12.157 --> 00:28:23.234
So let's just say last, oh,
50 to 300 millionish years,

00:28:23.234 --> 00:28:29.974
the ocean pH was 8.18.

00:28:29.974 --> 00:28:36.915
And today, it's 8.07.

00:28:36.915 --> 00:28:45.023
And the prediction is that
in 2100, it will be 7.8.

00:28:45.023 --> 00:28:47.325
Now, as we will see
in a little bit--

00:28:47.325 --> 00:28:49.094
and you say, what's pH?

00:28:49.094 --> 00:28:50.662
And many of you
probably already know,

00:28:50.662 --> 00:28:53.064
but I will tell you what
it is in a little bit.

00:28:53.064 --> 00:28:55.700
But because this is
a logarithmic thing,

00:28:55.700 --> 00:28:57.969
this is a lot, right?

00:28:57.969 --> 00:29:00.638
Today the ocean
is 25% more acidic

00:29:00.638 --> 00:29:03.441
than it's been in
300 millionish years.

00:29:03.441 --> 00:29:08.246
And in 2100, it will
be 126% more acidic.

00:29:08.246 --> 00:29:14.786
That's why they use 7.8 in the
experiments of the pteropods.

00:29:14.786 --> 00:29:18.256
OK, right.

00:29:18.256 --> 00:29:20.458
Why does this happen?

00:29:20.458 --> 00:29:22.660
Why is this molecule an acid?

00:29:22.660 --> 00:29:25.497
We're talking about
acidification of oceans.

00:29:25.497 --> 00:29:27.165
Why is this an acid
in the first place?

00:29:27.165 --> 00:29:28.666
What does it mean to be an acid?

00:29:28.666 --> 00:29:30.702
And that is the next topic.

00:29:30.702 --> 00:29:33.404
That is what I want
to talk about next.

00:29:33.404 --> 00:29:35.507
What is an acid?

00:29:35.507 --> 00:29:41.045
And an acid is something
that is very specific.

00:29:41.045 --> 00:29:44.549
It has a very definite
meaning to it.

00:29:44.549 --> 00:29:47.118
And I want to talk
about that today,

00:29:47.118 --> 00:29:50.021
and then we'll continue
after the break.

00:29:50.021 --> 00:29:55.460
So this molecule,
carbonic acid, is called

00:29:55.460 --> 00:29:58.997
an acid because of that proton.

00:29:58.997 --> 00:30:01.299
And you could feel it.

00:30:01.299 --> 00:30:03.434
You could feel it
in this whole thing.

00:30:03.434 --> 00:30:09.174
The proton is the thing that
caused the problem, right?

00:30:09.174 --> 00:30:13.411
An acid, this is an acid
because of the proton.

00:30:13.411 --> 00:30:19.851
So an acid, it's a dissolution
reaction, dissolution.

00:30:19.851 --> 00:30:21.319
It's what we've
been talking about.

00:30:21.319 --> 00:30:28.092
It's a dissolution
reaction that gives an H+.

00:30:31.496 --> 00:30:40.405
Now if I think about it as a
generic case, generic acid A,

00:30:40.405 --> 00:30:44.375
then the reaction looks
something like this.

00:30:44.375 --> 00:30:47.278
I'm going to call out
the acid and the proton

00:30:47.278 --> 00:30:50.281
that it gives separately.

00:30:50.281 --> 00:30:52.083
OK, so this is HA.

00:30:52.083 --> 00:30:55.353
So we do this because it
highlights that it's an acid,

00:30:55.353 --> 00:30:58.790
H+ in solution--

00:30:58.790 --> 00:31:04.295
and these can all be in
solution-- plus A- in solution.

00:31:04.295 --> 00:31:07.932
All right, H+, A-.

00:31:07.932 --> 00:31:10.201
Right, so I've
taken a proton off.

00:31:10.201 --> 00:31:12.003
You see it's right there.

00:31:12.003 --> 00:31:18.710
That's HA, HA, where A is
HCO3 and H is H. And then

00:31:18.710 --> 00:31:20.311
what I've done is
I've transferred

00:31:20.311 --> 00:31:21.646
the proton into solution.

00:31:21.646 --> 00:31:22.914
That's an acid.

00:31:22.914 --> 00:31:30.021
Now the thing is, you will
see a lot of places and people

00:31:30.021 --> 00:31:30.989
writing H+.

00:31:30.989 --> 00:31:34.459
And we will do that too, because
so many textbooks use H+.

00:31:34.459 --> 00:31:41.132
But what I want you to know
is that H+ is never a thing.

00:31:41.132 --> 00:31:42.634
We just write it that way.

00:31:42.634 --> 00:31:45.937
H+ is not stable in water.

00:31:45.937 --> 00:31:49.307
So the proper-- ah!

00:31:49.307 --> 00:31:54.646
The proper way to write this
general reaction would be HA

00:31:54.646 --> 00:31:59.217
plus H2O is going to go to--
and now I'm going to write

00:31:59.217 --> 00:32:02.186
the full, so the
full goes both ways--

00:32:02.186 --> 00:32:06.391
is going to go to H3O+ plus A-.

00:32:06.391 --> 00:32:07.225
[STUDENT SNEEZES]

00:32:07.225 --> 00:32:09.360
Gesundheit.

00:32:09.360 --> 00:32:12.931
This is what happens, because
H+ is not stable in water.

00:32:12.931 --> 00:32:15.566
But you will see H+
written all over the place.

00:32:15.566 --> 00:32:20.705
When you see H+, and if it's
in water, know that it's H3O+.

00:32:20.705 --> 00:32:23.041
That is what is stable.

00:32:23.041 --> 00:32:26.244
OK, H+ in water is not.

00:32:26.244 --> 00:32:27.679
Now, there's
something here that's

00:32:27.679 --> 00:32:29.914
important in terms
of terminology.

00:32:29.914 --> 00:32:33.384
And that is that these are--
notice that these are related.

00:32:33.384 --> 00:32:37.355
The HA and the A-
seem very related.

00:32:37.355 --> 00:32:41.526
And the H2O and the
H3O+ seem very related.

00:32:41.526 --> 00:32:45.296
Well, they are, because a
proton is the only difference

00:32:45.296 --> 00:32:46.698
between them.

00:32:46.698 --> 00:32:50.201
And so those are
called conjugate pairs.

00:32:50.201 --> 00:32:52.570
Conjugate, related,
conjugate pairs.

00:32:56.441 --> 00:33:01.045
OK, those are called
conjugate pairs.

00:33:01.045 --> 00:33:04.983
Now, back in the day, there
was a lot of interest.

00:33:04.983 --> 00:33:10.021
People knew about these kind
of liquids, mostly liquids,

00:33:10.021 --> 00:33:11.622
for a long time.

00:33:11.622 --> 00:33:15.026
And there was a lot of
experimentation before this.

00:33:15.026 --> 00:33:18.262
Let's see, well, there's these
general classes of materials.

00:33:18.262 --> 00:33:20.698
We're going to call
them A and B. Well,

00:33:20.698 --> 00:33:23.568
A seems to always
taste kind of sour.

00:33:23.568 --> 00:33:25.370
B is bitter.

00:33:25.370 --> 00:33:28.106
A reacts with
carbonates to make CO2.

00:33:28.106 --> 00:33:32.010
B reacts with fats and
soap to make soaps.

00:33:32.010 --> 00:33:32.944
One reacts with metals.

00:33:32.944 --> 00:33:33.711
The other doesn't.

00:33:33.711 --> 00:33:35.380
And by the way, if
we mix them together,

00:33:35.380 --> 00:33:37.949
we seem to always
get salt and water.

00:33:37.949 --> 00:33:41.519
This had been going on for
centuries, this investigation

00:33:41.519 --> 00:33:46.357
into properties of
acids and bases.

00:33:46.357 --> 00:33:47.792
But who was it that came along?

00:33:47.792 --> 00:33:50.194
Who was it that came along?

00:33:50.194 --> 00:33:52.397
Svante!

00:33:52.397 --> 00:33:54.932
Is there anything
you can't do, Svante?

00:33:54.932 --> 00:33:57.468
I ask you again.

00:33:57.468 --> 00:34:02.540
In the late 1800s, it was
Svante to the rescue who

00:34:02.540 --> 00:34:05.476
first proposed that
what is happening here

00:34:05.476 --> 00:34:07.945
is a dissolution reaction.

00:34:07.945 --> 00:34:10.614
That is what an
acid or a base is.

00:34:10.614 --> 00:34:14.018
It is that on the one
hand, you're given an H+,

00:34:14.018 --> 00:34:17.088
which is what I just said.

00:34:17.088 --> 00:34:22.226
But on the other hand, for
a base you're given a OH-.

00:34:22.226 --> 00:34:24.195
And it was Svante
Arrhenius who first

00:34:24.195 --> 00:34:27.398
put that down and first
conceived of acids and ba--

00:34:27.398 --> 00:34:28.866
are we snapping?

00:34:28.866 --> 00:34:30.400
Are we quiet
snapping for Svante?

00:34:30.400 --> 00:34:31.235
I love it.

00:34:31.235 --> 00:34:32.737
I've just recently
learned what that

00:34:32.737 --> 00:34:36.040
means, which makes me happy.

00:34:36.040 --> 00:34:38.109
Quiet snapping.

00:34:38.109 --> 00:34:40.043
It's instead of clapping.

00:34:40.043 --> 00:34:42.813
But you don't make noise, but
you still want to give props.

00:34:42.813 --> 00:34:44.047
I got it.

00:34:44.047 --> 00:34:44.849
I'm there.

00:34:44.849 --> 00:34:45.917
I'm right there.

00:34:45.917 --> 00:34:47.885
Now, OK.

00:34:47.885 --> 00:34:49.286
OK, now here's the thing.

00:34:49.286 --> 00:34:50.254
So we'll talk about it.

00:34:50.254 --> 00:34:55.960
So Arrhenius says bases give
OH ions in solution, and oh,

00:34:55.960 --> 00:34:56.994
acids give H+.

00:34:56.994 --> 00:35:00.531
But we know that that's H3O+.

00:35:00.531 --> 00:35:01.599
OK, we know that.

00:35:04.302 --> 00:35:07.872
Yeah, but the thing is
there's disillusion reactions.

00:35:07.872 --> 00:35:11.209
These things lead to really
small concentrations.

00:35:11.209 --> 00:35:12.810
And so Soren Sorensen.

00:35:15.379 --> 00:35:16.481
That is a beer bottle.

00:35:16.481 --> 00:35:20.585
By the way, his research was
funded by a beer company,

00:35:20.585 --> 00:35:23.888
and it was all about
the taste of beer.

00:35:23.888 --> 00:35:27.592
He called it
looking at proteins,

00:35:27.592 --> 00:35:29.360
but it was about beer.

00:35:29.360 --> 00:35:30.795
And what he realized,
he said, I've

00:35:30.795 --> 00:35:33.598
been doing all these
dissolution reactions of ions.

00:35:33.598 --> 00:35:34.832
I think Arrhenius was right.

00:35:34.832 --> 00:35:37.168
Ions are really
important, and we're

00:35:37.168 --> 00:35:38.736
trying to measure these things.

00:35:38.736 --> 00:35:42.039
But I'm sick of all these zeros.

00:35:42.039 --> 00:35:47.512
I don't like writing 0.0001
or whatever all the time.

00:35:47.512 --> 00:35:52.316
And oh, look at that, 1.3
times 10 to the minus fifth.

00:35:52.316 --> 00:35:54.785
That's not efficient.

00:35:54.785 --> 00:35:57.488
And so what Sorensen
did is he talked

00:35:57.488 --> 00:36:00.558
about how you've got the power.

00:36:00.558 --> 00:36:02.226
Now, what do I mean by power?

00:36:02.226 --> 00:36:04.028
So many things, right?

00:36:04.028 --> 00:36:10.835
But if I look at H+,
OK, H3O+, and I say it--

00:36:10.835 --> 00:36:17.808
let's suppose the concentration
is 0.00, oh boy, 00001,

00:36:17.808 --> 00:36:19.343
that's 10 to the minus 7.

00:36:22.380 --> 00:36:24.615
But Sorensen wasn't
happy with that.

00:36:24.615 --> 00:36:25.683
You simplified it.

00:36:25.683 --> 00:36:28.019
Wait, two, four-- no, it's not.

00:36:28.019 --> 00:36:32.089
OK, maybe it is now.

00:36:32.089 --> 00:36:33.658
He said, well, 10
to the minus seventh

00:36:33.658 --> 00:36:38.129
is still four characters
I got to write, four.

00:36:38.129 --> 00:36:41.632
So he went to the math
department at his place

00:36:41.632 --> 00:36:44.435
and he said, what else can be--

00:36:44.435 --> 00:36:47.271
I want to talk about
the power of these ions.

00:36:47.271 --> 00:36:51.142
And they're like, power,
well, that's the logarithm.

00:36:51.142 --> 00:36:54.612
And so if you take the
log, it's the power

00:36:54.612 --> 00:37:00.952
of hydrogen. Power of
hydrogen is the pH.

00:37:00.952 --> 00:37:02.453
And that's simply minus the log.

00:37:02.453 --> 00:37:04.789
They didn't want
negative numbers either.

00:37:04.789 --> 00:37:07.491
Oh, but you can
have negative pH.

00:37:07.491 --> 00:37:09.393
You can.

00:37:09.393 --> 00:37:09.894
Why?

00:37:09.894 --> 00:37:14.699
Because this is the
definition of pH.

00:37:14.699 --> 00:37:19.337
Or you could do pOH, if
you're going with Arrhenius.

00:37:19.337 --> 00:37:22.573
So the power of
hydrogen is the pH.

00:37:22.573 --> 00:37:28.379
The pOH would be minus log
of the concentration of OH-.

00:37:28.379 --> 00:37:29.013
That's the pOH.

00:37:29.013 --> 00:37:32.750
But pH is the one
that we most often

00:37:32.750 --> 00:37:36.621
see to describe whether
something is acidic or basic.

00:37:39.123 --> 00:37:42.793
And so now you see, well,
OK, well, this is a pH.

00:37:42.793 --> 00:37:43.794
Now this is easy, right?

00:37:43.794 --> 00:37:45.496
It's a pH of 7.

00:37:45.496 --> 00:37:50.034
And if the concentration were
0.1, it would be a pH of 1,

00:37:50.034 --> 00:37:53.504
minus log, the
power of hydrogen,

00:37:53.504 --> 00:37:57.508
the log, literally the log.

00:37:57.508 --> 00:37:59.944
OK, now if you look
at scales of pH,

00:37:59.944 --> 00:38:02.780
and it's a really
fun thing to do, then

00:38:02.780 --> 00:38:05.549
what you find is that usually,
almost always actually,

00:38:05.549 --> 00:38:07.685
you'll see the scale
ranging from 0 to 14.

00:38:07.685 --> 00:38:10.388
That's what Sorensen
originally decided.

00:38:10.388 --> 00:38:12.657
I'm going to talk about
these ions in solution.

00:38:12.657 --> 00:38:14.325
I don't want to write
it out every time.

00:38:14.325 --> 00:38:16.093
I'm just going to
have a simple scale.

00:38:16.093 --> 00:38:18.696
We'll take the minus
log, and there you go.

00:38:18.696 --> 00:38:21.565
Yeah, but you can see that
if the concentration is

00:38:21.565 --> 00:38:24.502
more than one here, then
you could have negative pHs.

00:38:24.502 --> 00:38:27.738
There's nothing special
about zero here.

00:38:27.738 --> 00:38:30.174
It just so happens
that most things,

00:38:30.174 --> 00:38:32.143
certainly that he was
playing with at the time,

00:38:32.143 --> 00:38:33.277
were in this scale.

00:38:33.277 --> 00:38:35.446
And here is a few
things that we know.

00:38:35.446 --> 00:38:36.247
There it is, right?

00:38:36.247 --> 00:38:38.883
OK, don't play with
that one, but play

00:38:38.883 --> 00:38:40.484
with lemon juice, vinegar.

00:38:40.484 --> 00:38:41.719
By the way, vinegar--

00:38:41.719 --> 00:38:44.722
OK, wine is around 3 and 1/2.

00:38:44.722 --> 00:38:45.923
Vinegar is around 2.

00:38:49.060 --> 00:38:53.164
The word vinegar is
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]..

00:38:53.164 --> 00:38:55.266
It's eager wine.

00:38:55.266 --> 00:38:56.367
That's it, right?

00:38:56.367 --> 00:39:00.204
But it's because of the
acidity, the acidity.

00:39:00.204 --> 00:39:01.272
Well, it's more than that.

00:39:01.272 --> 00:39:08.045
But it's the acidity is changing
by orders of magnitude, power.

00:39:08.045 --> 00:39:10.581
And then you can go back and
you see, but OK, there's coffee.

00:39:10.581 --> 00:39:12.049
I like that a lot.

00:39:12.049 --> 00:39:15.152
And blood.

00:39:15.152 --> 00:39:20.291
By the way, by the way,
talk about ocean acidity,

00:39:20.291 --> 00:39:23.461
blood has a very
narrow range of pH.

00:39:23.461 --> 00:39:27.331
And if you change the pH of
your blood by more than 0.2,

00:39:27.331 --> 00:39:29.934
it is very likely
to lead to death.

00:39:29.934 --> 00:39:30.968
So just think about that.

00:39:30.968 --> 00:39:33.738
That's what we're
doing to the ocean.

00:39:33.738 --> 00:39:36.974
OK, anyway, that was an aside.

00:39:36.974 --> 00:39:41.679
Seawater, oh, pH 8.07
currently and dropping.

00:39:41.679 --> 00:39:43.147
OK, baking soda, ammonia.

00:39:43.147 --> 00:39:48.786
So this is very powerful,
no pun intended actually.

00:39:48.786 --> 00:39:51.822
Now, these are
dissociation reactions.

00:39:51.822 --> 00:39:53.257
These are dissolution reactions.

00:39:53.257 --> 00:39:58.996
I'm taking an acid, and I'm
dissociating it in water.

00:39:58.996 --> 00:40:02.566
And so you have the equilibrium
constant for that dissociation,

00:40:02.566 --> 00:40:03.734
right?

00:40:03.734 --> 00:40:08.239
And so if you go
back to that, that's

00:40:08.239 --> 00:40:10.007
still going to matter here.

00:40:10.007 --> 00:40:12.877
And that's going to be
important in thinking

00:40:12.877 --> 00:40:16.614
about what an acid is.

00:40:16.614 --> 00:40:18.249
Because, again, why is this it?

00:40:18.249 --> 00:40:19.750
Well, because there's
these protons.

00:40:19.750 --> 00:40:21.118
Well, how many of
them are there?

00:40:21.118 --> 00:40:23.320
It depends on how it dissolved.

00:40:23.320 --> 00:40:26.757
Where did it find
its equilibrium?

00:40:26.757 --> 00:40:30.394
And so for some of
them, like I just wrote,

00:40:30.394 --> 00:40:36.333
we just talked
about HCl plus H2O.

00:40:36.333 --> 00:40:43.107
That went to H3O+ plus Cl-.

00:40:43.107 --> 00:40:52.450
OK, now the thing is, if I had
0.1 moles per liter of HCl,

00:40:52.450 --> 00:40:58.689
then it's going to lead to a
concentration of H3O+ of around

00:40:58.689 --> 00:41:03.961
0.1 moles per liter,
because it's nearly full,

00:41:03.961 --> 00:41:09.099
nearly full dissociation.

00:41:09.099 --> 00:41:10.067
What do I mean by that?

00:41:10.067 --> 00:41:12.436
Well, I mean that--
yeah, OK, so you'll often

00:41:12.436 --> 00:41:16.106
see for an acid like
that, one arrow.

00:41:16.106 --> 00:41:20.778
Well, we know that in reality,
there's another arrow there.

00:41:20.778 --> 00:41:26.484
But see, here the acid
dissociation constant is huge.

00:41:26.484 --> 00:41:30.020
So now we have a thing
for the equilibrium

00:41:30.020 --> 00:41:33.457
of an acid, which
forms by dissociating

00:41:33.457 --> 00:41:36.694
the acid into its ions.

00:41:36.694 --> 00:41:41.365
And this is-- well, in this
case, it's 10 to the sixth.

00:41:41.365 --> 00:41:45.035
It's enormous, which means
that the equilibrium lies very,

00:41:45.035 --> 00:41:46.737
very, very, very far over.

00:41:46.737 --> 00:41:48.539
And so oftentimes
you'll see if it's

00:41:48.539 --> 00:41:52.510
going to strongly dissociate
it, like sodium chloride did,

00:41:52.510 --> 00:41:54.879
then you'll sometimes see it
written as just one arrow.

00:41:54.879 --> 00:41:59.350
But there's a little
bit going back as well.

00:41:59.350 --> 00:42:01.785
That is another
equilibrium constant.

00:42:01.785 --> 00:42:03.888
And we will talk about
Ka a little later

00:42:03.888 --> 00:42:06.957
and probably pick
up on it next week.

00:42:06.957 --> 00:42:09.727
OK, so near full dissociation.

00:42:09.727 --> 00:42:14.865
Now, the other thing that
can happen that's important

00:42:14.865 --> 00:42:16.233
is water can do both.

00:42:19.403 --> 00:42:20.771
Let's see, where should I go?

00:42:20.771 --> 00:42:24.775
I'll go back over here.

00:42:24.775 --> 00:42:29.046
So water, see that
picture there?

00:42:29.046 --> 00:42:30.614
They got it wrong, but it's OK.

00:42:30.614 --> 00:42:32.583
They got it right,
because we all get along.

00:42:32.583 --> 00:42:35.052
If you want to put H+, fine.

00:42:35.052 --> 00:42:38.155
But you know that
it's actually H3O+.

00:42:38.155 --> 00:42:41.058
OK, but you can
put H+, no problem.

00:42:41.058 --> 00:42:44.461
OK, now there it is.

00:42:44.461 --> 00:42:47.898
And you can see that what
water can do is it can do both.

00:42:47.898 --> 00:42:50.734
And that turns out to
be extremely important.

00:42:50.734 --> 00:42:56.340
So if I take water and
I mix it with water,

00:42:56.340 --> 00:43:09.687
ah ha, then you can get a
combination of these ions,

00:43:09.687 --> 00:43:13.791
oh, plus a whole bunch of water.

00:43:13.791 --> 00:43:16.327
So this would be
like dissociation.

00:43:16.327 --> 00:43:21.365
Now, the word for this,
water can turn into either.

00:43:21.365 --> 00:43:23.233
It can be basic or acidic.

00:43:23.233 --> 00:43:28.405
It can deliver OH- or H3O+.

00:43:28.405 --> 00:43:32.409
That's called autoionization,
and it also has a special word.

00:43:32.409 --> 00:43:38.782
Water is called
amphoteric, amphoteric.

00:43:38.782 --> 00:43:47.057
Now, that means that
the same thing can

00:43:47.057 --> 00:43:56.867
be both, both acid or a base.

00:43:56.867 --> 00:43:59.103
It can act as either.

00:43:59.103 --> 00:44:01.939
So if you take pure water--

00:44:01.939 --> 00:44:03.440
so let's see.

00:44:03.440 --> 00:44:13.484
If I take pure water
and [GROANS] and I look

00:44:13.484 --> 00:44:19.857
at the concentrations
of these, so H3O+,

00:44:19.857 --> 00:44:21.291
it's all happening
from the water.

00:44:21.291 --> 00:44:22.226
There's nothing in it.

00:44:22.226 --> 00:44:23.527
I didn't add anything to it.

00:44:23.527 --> 00:44:25.429
It's just pure water, H3O+.

00:44:25.429 --> 00:44:28.465
So that means that if
that reaction happens,

00:44:28.465 --> 00:44:31.869
it's going to generate
the same number.

00:44:31.869 --> 00:44:36.607
So the H3O+ would equal the
concentration of the OH-.

00:44:36.607 --> 00:44:40.544
And in pure water,
that is a value

00:44:40.544 --> 00:44:45.115
that is equal to 1 times 10
to the minus seventh moles

00:44:45.115 --> 00:44:54.158
per liter at 25C, at 25C.

00:44:54.158 --> 00:44:56.293
And so now we have yet another.

00:44:56.293 --> 00:44:58.429
That's neutral.

00:44:58.429 --> 00:45:02.700
You can understand
that that's neutral,

00:45:02.700 --> 00:45:04.735
because you have the same--

00:45:04.735 --> 00:45:10.007
if an acid is H+ or H3O+ in
solution and a base is OH-,

00:45:10.007 --> 00:45:16.146
and I've made the same number,
then the acidity and basicity

00:45:16.146 --> 00:45:17.414
are neutralizing one other.

00:45:17.414 --> 00:45:18.782
So that's neutral.

00:45:18.782 --> 00:45:23.821
And in fact, you can
see that that pH is 7.

00:45:23.821 --> 00:45:27.624
Oh, and we can even write the
equilibrium constant for water,

00:45:27.624 --> 00:45:35.999
which is going to be those
guys, H3O+ times OH-,

00:45:35.999 --> 00:45:42.740
which is equal to 10
to the negative 14th,

00:45:42.740 --> 00:45:46.343
because that's the dissociation
reaction for water.

00:45:46.343 --> 00:45:49.780
Water plus water goes to
those ions in solution.

00:45:49.780 --> 00:45:53.050
We ignore the water,
and then we're

00:45:53.050 --> 00:45:55.185
left with this same
thing we've been

00:45:55.185 --> 00:45:58.288
doing, 10 to the minus 14th.

00:45:58.288 --> 00:46:00.991
Now, that takes us--

00:46:00.991 --> 00:46:02.593
those are neutral.

00:46:02.593 --> 00:46:06.096
And so speaking
of neutral, that's

00:46:06.096 --> 00:46:07.931
where we're going to go next.

00:46:07.931 --> 00:46:12.035
